# Workbench canvas data

The Workbench is the canvas `workbench.canvas.tsx` in this workspace canvases
folder. Persisted data is `workbench.canvas.data.json` next to it. Do not
change `hubTask` or `hubPage`; they are the task and tab the user has open.

Edit the canvas source in `~/Projects/software-engineering-workbench/`. A user hook copies
that file into each workspace. Do not treat the workspace copy as the source.

## Tasks

The sidebar lists tasks. Each task has its own Feature specification (tables
`user-stories`, `acceptance-criteria`, `functional-requirements`,
`non-functional-requirements`), Architecture (mermaid diagrams), UI elements
(table `ui-elements`), Functional elements (table `react-hooks`), Unit tests
(table `unit-tests`), and Integration tests (table `integration-tests`) tabs.

- `tasks` is the ordered list of `{ "id", "name" }`.
- `taskSpecs.<taskId>` holds the spec of one task: `specColumns` and
  `specRows`, each keyed by table id.
- `taskArchitecture.<taskId>` holds the Architecture diagrams of the task.

Find a task by its `name` in `tasks` and use its `id` for the other keys.
When the user does not name a task, use the one in `hubTask`.

### Spec tables

You and the user both write `taskSpecs.<taskId>.specRows`. Every cell is
open to both.

```json
{
  "taskSpecs": {
    "<taskId>": {
      "specColumns": {
        "<tableId>": [{ "id": "<columnId>", "name": "Story", "kind": "text" }]
      },
      "specRows": {
        "<tableId>": [{ "id": "row-<unique>", "done": false, "cells": [""] }]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- `cells` is an array of strings in the order of `specColumns.<tableId>`.
  When a table has no `specColumns` entry, the canvas uses the defaults in
  `seedColumns` of the canvas source. Write `specColumns.<tableId>` in full
  when you change the columns of such a table.
- A short `cells` array reads as empty in the missing positions.
- `kind` is `text`, `code`, `selection`, or `multi-selection`. A `code` cell
  holds TypeScript source.
- Read all rows of the task for context before you implement it.
- You may fill or change any cell, and add rows. Give each new row an id of
  the form `row-<unique>`, `done: false`, and a `cells` array that matches
  the column order.
- Change `tasks`, `specColumns`, row ids, the Done checkbox,
  or delete rows when the user asks for it or the task needs it. Tell the
  user what you changed.

### Selection and multi-selection cells

A column `kind` of `selection` or `multi-selection` stores JSON in the same
string cell as other kinds.

- `selection`: one object, or `""` when empty.
- `multi-selection`: a JSON array of those objects, or `""` when empty.

```json
{ "tableId": "ui-elements", "rowId": "row-abc123" }
```

`tableId` is a table on the UI elements tab (`ui-elements`) or the
Functional elements tab (`react-hooks`). If those tabs gain more tables,
those table ids are valid too. `rowId` is the `id` of a row in
`taskSpecs.<taskId>.specRows.<tableId>`. Invalid JSON and leftover free
text read as empty in the UI.

## Unit tests and Integration tests

Tables `unit-tests` and `integration-tests` follow the spec table rules
above. Default columns are text: `Components` then `Description`. The Done
checkbox is part of the row, not a stored column.

- Write **Components** as the code-level parts under test, in plain text
  when that column is Text. If the user changed the column to Selection or
  Multi-selection, write the JSON refs described above. Do not invent
  `tableId` or `rowId` values; copy them from existing UI element and
  functional element rows.
- Write **Description** as what must be true, in plain English. Do not name
  a test framework, runner, or assertion library. When you later write the
  test files, follow that description and use the stack the project already
  uses.

## Architecture

`taskArchitecture.<taskId>` is an ordered list of diagrams:

```json
{
  "taskArchitecture": {
    "<taskId>": [
      {
        "id": "arch-<unique>",
        "title": "Untitled diagram",
        "source": "<mermaid text>",
        "renderedSource": "<mermaid text the svg was made from>",
        "svg": "<svg string>",
        "renderError": ""
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

A missing key means the task has no diagrams. Missing fields read as empty.

- Read `source` of every diagram for context when you implement the task.
- You and the user both may edit `title` and `source`. Add or delete a
  diagram only when the user asks. A new diagram gets an id of the form
  `arch-<unique>` and an empty `source`.
- After you change any `source`, run
  `node ~/Projects/software-engineering-workbench/scripts/render-architecture.mjs <data file>`
  so the picture matches the text. `<data file>` is the
  `workbench.canvas.data.json` of this workspace.
- When the user asks to render the Architecture diagrams of a task (the
  **Save changes** button in the canvas sends that request), run the same
  command. Pass the task id as a second argument to limit the run to that
  task.
- Never write `svg`, `renderedSource`, or `renderError` by hand. Only the
  script writes them.
- The script needs `npm install` to have been run once in
  `~/Projects/software-engineering-workbench`. If it fails with a missing
  `beautiful-mermaid` package, tell the user to run it.
